From: Macron <macron@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 03:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] server: Write the periodic registry save from a child process
 on macOS.

Thirty seconds after it starts, and every thirty seconds in which a
registry branch was modified, the server writes that branch to disk,
whole. It has one thread: while it writes, every thread of every process
that asks it anything waits. A prefix's system.reg is several megabytes.
On a MacBook Air M3 the save took 63, 83 and 152 ms in three starts of
Deadlock (one turn of the main loop each, no request in it: Macron's
patch 0020 logs such turns), 20 s after the game's window. Wine's audio
timer sets its event through the server, so the game's sound thread was
woken that late: 162 to 3,270 frames of sound missing in one gap in each
of the three starts, and the game's own "Audio device starved" beside
it. It is the stall that every start of the day had in its first half
minute and that Macron's sound reserve (0016) covered when it was
shorter than 58 ms.

Now a child process writes the files, from the copy of the registry that
fork() gives it, and the server goes on. The branches are marked clean
when the child starts. If the child fails, they are marked modified
again when it is reaped, so the next save or the one at exit writes
them. One child at a time: a save that comes due while one is writing
waits for the next round. The save at exit waits for the child first, so
that an older copy cannot land on top of a newer one.

On macOS the server had no children and its SIGCHLD callback asserted;
it reaps now.
---
